From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 18:43:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] indexeddb: Evict a cached page that holds back another page's
 request or transaction

A document in the back/forward cache never finishes its IndexedDB
transactions or upgrades: the event loop holds back its tasks, and with
them the results on its connections' result channels, so script never
commits. A page that wrote on pagehide (the pattern of WPT
back-forward-cache-open-transaction) and then went into the cache held
back, until the user went back to it or it was evicted for another
reason:

- a delete or upgrade of the next page, if the cached page closed its
  connection: the closing connection waits for the write's transaction,
  gets no versionchange event, and the request fired nothing at all;
- a transaction of the next page on the same store, which the scheduler
  holds behind the earlier writer;
- every request queued behind an open whose upgrade runs in the cached
  page.

Do what Chromium's DisallowInactiveClient does: the backend sends
ConnectionEvent::EvictIfInactive to the connection that holds the others
back, and script, through the task without a pipeline that versionchange
events take, evicts the connection's document if it isn't fully active
and ignores the event otherwise (workers ignore it). The backend sends
it

- for a delete or upgrade that, once its versionchange events were
  reported, waits for a connection whose close is pending (once per
  request);
- for a transaction that can't start, to the connections of the
  transactions that keep it from starting (once per waiting
  transaction, also for a commit without requests);
- for an open request whose upgrade runs while a request is queued
  behind it.

A cached page that nothing waits for is still restored
(back-forward-cache-open-transaction).

Also fire blocked for a connection whose close was already pending when
the request fired its versionchange events, as the spec and Chromium do,
so that the waiting page hears something: Chromium's IDBDatabase reports
VersionChangeIgnored for a close-pending connection, which makes its
backend send blocked. A connection that the page closes in its
versionchange handler still doesn't block while its transactions finish,
as in Chromium and Gecko.

The fork test back-forward-cache-held-back.window.js covers the four
cases with RemoteContextHelper: a pagehide write and close followed by a
delete and by an upgrade, a pagehide write without close followed by a
read, and an open on pagehide whose upgrade a later open waits for.

Validation: host `cargo nextest run -p servo-storage -p servo-storage-traits` at this commit: 163 passed, 1 skipped (new: test_indexeddb_delete_blocked_by_a_closing_connection_asks_it_to_evict, test_indexeddb_held_back_transaction_asks_the_holder_to_evict, test_indexeddb_request_queued_behind_an_upgrade_asks_it_to_evict; the close-pending and request-order tests now expect blocked and the eviction requests; the first run had the known webstorage clear_data_for_sites_local timeout, which passed 20 of 20 alone); the new /_mozilla/IndexedDB/back-forward-cache-held-back.window.html passes 4 of 4 on host SM and QuickJS and times out on a binary whose script ignores EvictIfInactive (P5.3/review-fixes/bfc-neg.raw); the P5 review's probes, two runs each (P5.3/review-fixes/probe): a delete after a pagehide write and close gives blocked old=1 and deleted old=1, an upgrade gives blocked, upgradeneeded old=1 and success version=2, a read after a pagehide write without close completes with undefined, where all three stalled before, and the controls without the write are unchanged; IndexedDB/back-forward-cache-open-transaction, whose cached page nothing waits for, is still restored; on the stack tip host SM IndexedDB/ 4,484 of 4,511 in 3 of 3 back-to-back runs and QuickJS 4,484 in 14 of 15 runs (one whole-test timeout of idb-explicit-commit.any.html, 15 of 15 alone), every other result as expected, /_mozilla/IndexedDB/ 318 of 318 SM and 316 of 318 QuickJS (interface lists, as before), /workers/ 337 tests and html/browsers/browsing-the-web/back-forward-cache/ 23 tests as expected on SM; DAYU200 (QuickJS + RDB) IndexedDB/ 4,461 and 4,470 of 4,511 in two runs (P5.3: 4,468 to 4,476), unexpected only file_support (WebDriver send_keys) and idb-partitioned-coverage (iframe slowness), as before, /_mozilla/IndexedDB/ 316 of 316 with both back/forward cache fork tests 4 of 4; device unit tests RDB only 168 passed, 3 skipped, both backends 304 passed, 4 skipped
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; the agent designed and implemented the eviction requests and the blocked change, wrote the integration and fork tests, re-ran the review's probes and measured host and board)
